#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509013} :activity/id #uuid "ce408938-6ff2-5ee9-9af3-b3e6dc563bd5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050429 :topic/id #uuid "486a09f3-45ae-5574-b216-0a329beb1dea" :topic/math-academy-id 757} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509011 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c017070-88fd-5d41-924a-dd54f0531459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244703} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac6a7f4-b7e5-5aab-882f-f2ec82413511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244981} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3708f5-187e-5b74-bb7c-fb6773b51adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245410} {:answer/id #uuid "b691b5c9-8cce-5aa4-9d4c-8759d0a10381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245946} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2dabbb-0466-5a5e-8205-efb8aaaa4a25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245985}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244981} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a0be498-1922-5ad5-b70c-3159737e4b82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b6b933a9-c0e8-5275-ae25-ac03d96707b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-112875" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-4?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-4$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-4}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-3$ does not take the above form. The value $-3$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{3}{1},$ and the numerator $(3)$ is not a factor of $-4.$"} :step/id #uuid "5496c088-fa95-5052-8432-a8ee6731ca57" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509023}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509013 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508948 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "000d0b8b-482a-53a8-87ce-fe362b914282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508388} {:answer/id #uuid "3334f6ee-ea48-5b27-93c8-f3725a806b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508466} {:answer/id #uuid "50c09e5c-1c9d-53b7-9773-98ff2805aa40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508510} {:answer/id #uuid "67e31522-060a-5e95-9560-1ac9b15d8ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508543} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e893b0-43f9-5fae-8bb4-0d4cfd2cce16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508745}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508388} :answer-field/id #uuid "e68114ce-13fb-5bcc-bd0f-4d1c50824307" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "87021664-1460-517e-b0da-ae45fc0bbb9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-83831" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}+x+1,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $1,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $6$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 3=6.$)"} :step/id #uuid "58d4a216-d386-5004-acc6-1259a07e1494" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509011}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509023 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b16f84b-0fdd-5a3e-8aa0-dc62596d443c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300664}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300664} :answer-field/id #uuid "410552d6-05ce-5fcd-88f6-0b3a368b5a06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fd8abfc9-fc42-5fbe-8914-ffe40a38eb8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-323218" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-7$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{7},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 7.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $6$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 3=6.$)"} :step/id #uuid "98570077-079e-5420-bc17-984e3fb96b3b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509041}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "290c0af0-b447-58fd-8761-71af3c0f95df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300157}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300157} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ea9a655-49ca-533c-8cb7-421e176fbf73" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301433} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "060cda53-8668-5be5-8aef-25b100d05754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299961}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299961} :answer-field/id #uuid "80e37fe1-bd7c-51e8-ba48-a531ae7460a1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301635} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b6ea38-f33a-5329-8a75-bcbcd85f7f0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300028} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7f8d121-1dd4-5d54-b226-07216b17964f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3518da80-0db9-569e-b980-2b5efc57c5f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-323819" :question/problem "The polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-9x-21$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-3,-2).$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-21$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-21}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 7,\\,\\pm 21,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(-3,-2)$ is $-\\frac{7}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{7}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{7}{3}$ | $3$ | $7$ | $-9$ | $-21$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-7$ | $0$ | $21$ |\n|  | $3$ | $0$ | $-9$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{7}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{7}{3})(3{x}^{2}-9).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{7}{3})(3{x}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{7}{3})({x}^{2}-3) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{7}{3})(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=-\\frac{7}{3},$ $x=-\\sqrt{3},$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "c5006e3f-438a-526e-aab3-e8ec1d08a5ad"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d17d593-2ae4-53d7-a4b5-82e61d1bfa57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492236} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1ef24a4-cbc2-5e7b-841f-6bdc135e149a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "12d33f18-863a-5b25-ace7-f97482164c70" :question/math-academy-id "q-323572" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-x-15$ has a root in the interval $(1,4),$ the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x)$ equals{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-15$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }15}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 5,\\,\\pm 15.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(1,4)$ is $3.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-15$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $6$ | $15$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-3)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)({x}^{2}+2x+5).\n$$\n\nNow we must see if the polynomial $g(x)={x}^{2}+2x+5$ has real roots. For this, we must find the value of its discriminant $D.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-4(1)(5) \\\\  & =4-20 \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0,$ the polynomial $g(x)$ doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "d86879e5-509b-538b-b3ff-c28fdb97936e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509036}}] :activity/title "The Rational Roots Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508386}]}
